  <subsection title="Simplify Expressions witha1na1n">
    <p>Rational exponents are another way of writing expressions with radicals. When we use rational exponents, we can apply the properties of exponents to simplify expressions.</p>
    <p>The Power Property for Exponents says that $$\left( a^{m} \right)^{n} = a^{m \cdot n}$$ when m and n are whole numbers. Let’s assume we are now not limited to whole numbers.</p>
    <p>Suppose we want to find a number p such that $$\left( 8^{p} \right)^{3} = 8.$$ We will use the Power Property of Exponents to find the value of p.</p>
    <p>$$\begin{array}{cccccrll} &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \left( 8^{p} \right)^{3} &amp; = &amp; 8 \\ \text{Multiply the exponents on the left.} &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; 8^{3p} &amp; = &amp; 8 \\ \text{Write the exponent 1 on the right.} &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; 8^{3p} &amp; = &amp; 8^{1} \\ \text{Since the bases are the same, the exponents must be equal.} &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; {3p} &amp; = &amp; 1 \\ {\text{Solve for}\ p.} &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; p &amp; = &amp; \frac{1}{3} \end{array}$$</p>
    <p>So $$\left( 8^{\frac{1}{3}} \right)^{3} = 8.$$ But we know also $$\left( \sqrt[3]{8} \right)^{3} = 8.$$ Then it must be that $$8^{\frac{1}{3}} = \sqrt[3]{8}.$$</p>
    <p>This same logic can be used for any positive integer exponent n to show that $$a^{\frac{1}{n}} = \sqrt[n]{a}.$$</p>
    <note type="Rational Exponenta1na1n">
      <p>If $$\sqrt[n]{a}$$ is a real number and $$n \geq 2,$$ then</p>
      <equation>$$a^{\frac{1}{n}} = \sqrt[n]{a}$$</equation>
    </note>
    <p>The denominator of the rational exponent is the index of the radical.</p>
    <p>There will be times when working with expressions will be easier if you use rational exponents and times when it will be easier if you use radicals. In the first few examples, you’ll practice converting expressions between these two notations.</p>
    <example number="8.26">
      <problem>
        <p>Write as a radical expression: ⓐ $$x^{\frac{1}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$y^{\frac{1}{3}}$$ ⓒ $$z^{\frac{1}{4}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>We want to write each expression in the form $$\sqrt[n]{a}.$$</p>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Illustrates the conversion of the rational exponent x^(1/2) to its radical form, sqrt(x), explaining the rule for the radical index.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu} x^{\frac{1}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The denominator of the rational exponent is 2, sothe index of the radical is 2. We do not show theindex when it is 2.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu}\sqrt{x}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the conversion from fractional exponents to radical form, specifically demonstrating y^(1/3) as the cube root of y.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{126mu} y^{\frac{1}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The denominator of the exponent is 3, so theindex is 3.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{126mu}\sqrt[3]{y}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the conversion of a fractional exponent to its equivalent radical form, focusing on how the exponent's denominator determines the radical index.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{126mu} z^{\frac{1}{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The denominator of the exponent is 4, so theindex is 4.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{126mu}\sqrt[4]{z}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.51">
      <p>Write as a radical expression: ⓐ $$t^{\frac{1}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$m^{\frac{1}{3}}$$ ⓒ $$r^{\frac{1}{4}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.52">
      <p>Write as a radial expression: ⓐ $$b^{\frac{1}{6}}$$ ⓑ $$z^{\frac{1}{5}}$$ ⓒ $$p^{\frac{1}{4}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>In the next example, we will write each radical using a rational exponent. It is important to use parentheses around the entire expression in the radicand since the entire expression is raised to the rational power.</p>
    <example number="8.27">
      <problem>
        <p>Write with a rational exponent: ⓐ $$\sqrt{5y}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[3]{4x}$$ ⓒ $$3\sqrt[4]{5z}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>We want to write each radical in the form $$a^{\frac{1}{n}}.$$</p>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Shows the conversion of a square root radical expression to its equivalent exponential form, with explanatory notes.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\sqrt{5y}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>No index is shown, so it is 2.The denominator of the exponent will be 2.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( {5y} \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Put parentheses around the entireexpression $$5y.$$</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates converting a radical expression to its equivalent fractional exponent form, with an explanation of the underlying process.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu}\sqrt[3]{4x}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The index is 3, so the denominator of theexponent is 3. Include parentheses $$(4x).$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu}\left( {4x} \right)^{\frac{1}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table summary="Illustrates converting a radical expression to its rational exponent form, with detailed explanations.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 3\ \sqrt[4]{5z}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The index is 4, so the denominator of theexponent is 4. Put parentheses only aroundthe $$5z$$ since 3 is not under the radical sign.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 3\left( {5z} \right)^{\frac{1}{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.53">
      <p>Write with a rational exponent: ⓐ $$\sqrt{10m}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[5]{3n}$$ ⓒ $$3\sqrt[4]{6y}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.54">
      <p>Write with a rational exponent: ⓐ $$\sqrt[7]{3k}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt[4]{5j}$$ ⓒ $$8\sqrt[3]{2a}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>In the next example, you may find it easier to simplify the expressions if you rewrite them as radicals first.</p>
    <example number="8.28">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$25^{\frac{1}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$64^{\frac{1}{3}}$$ ⓒ $$256^{\frac{1}{4}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Steps to simplify a fractional exponent: rewriting 25^(1/2) as a square root and finding its value.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu} 25^{\frac{1}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as a square root.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu}\sqrt{25}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{198mu} 5$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the step-by-step simplification of 64 raised to the power of one-third, demonstrating its conversion to a cube root and final evaluation.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{153mu} 64^{\frac{1}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as a cube root.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{153mu}\sqrt[3]{64}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Recognize 64 is a perfect cube.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{153mu}\sqrt[3]{4^{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{153mu} 4$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the step-by-step simplification of the mathematical expression 256 to the power of 1/4, demonstrating its transformation into a fourth root and final numerical evaluation.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 256^{\frac{1}{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as a fourth root.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\sqrt[4]{256}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Recognize 256 is a perfect fourth power.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\sqrt[4]{4^{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 4$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.55">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$36^{\frac{1}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$8^{\frac{1}{3}}$$ ⓒ $$16^{\frac{1}{4}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.56">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$100^{\frac{1}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$27^{\frac{1}{3}}$$ ⓒ $$81^{\frac{1}{4}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Be careful of the placement of the negative signs in the next example. We will need to use the property $$a^{\text{−}n} = \frac{1}{a^{n}}$$ in one case.</p>
    <example number="8.29">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$(-16)^{\frac{1}{4}}$$ ⓑ $$\text{−}16^{\frac{1}{4}}$$ ⓒ $$(16)^{- \frac{1}{4}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the step-by-step simplification of the expression (-16)^(1/4), demonstrating the process to determine there is no real solution.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{162mu}(-16)^{\frac{1}{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as a fourth root.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{162mu}\sqrt[4]{-16}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{162mu}\sqrt[4]{(-2)^{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{162mu}\text{No real solution.}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of the mathematical expression -16^(1/4), detailing each transformation to reach the final result.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{99mu}\text{−}16^{\frac{1}{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The exponent only applies to the 16.Rewrite as a fouth root.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{99mu}\text{−}\sqrt[4]{16}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite 16 as $$2^{4}.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{99mu}\text{−}\sqrt[4]{2^{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{99mu}-2$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table summary="Demonstrates the step-by-step simplification of (16)^(-1/4), converting it to 1/2 using exponent and root properties.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu}(16)^{- \frac{1}{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite using the property $$a^{\text{−}n} = \frac{1}{a^{n}}.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu}\frac{1}{(16)^{\frac{1}{4}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite as a fourth root.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu}\frac{1}{\sqrt[4]{16}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite 16 as $$2^{4}.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu}\frac{1}{\sqrt[4]{2^{4}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu}\frac{1}{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.57">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(-64)}^{- \frac{1}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$\text{−}64^{\frac{1}{2}}$$ ⓒ $${(64)}^{- \frac{1}{2}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.58">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $${(-256)}^{\frac{1}{4}}$$ ⓑ $$\text{−}256^{\frac{1}{4}}$$ ⓒ $$(256)^{- \frac{1}{4}}.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Simplify Expressions withamnamn">
    <p>We can look at $$a^{\frac{m}{n}}$$ in two ways. Remember the Power Property tells us to multiply the exponents and so $$\left( a^{\frac{1}{n}} \right)^{m}$$ and $$\left( a^{m} \right)^{{}^{\frac{1}{n}}}$$ both equal $$a^{\frac{m}{n}}.$$ If we write these expressions in radical form, we get</p>
    <equation>$$a^{\frac{m}{n}} = \left( a^{\frac{1}{n}} \right)^{m} = \left( \sqrt[n]{a} \right)^{m}\quad\text{and}\quad a^{\frac{m}{n}} = \left( a^{m} \right)^{{}^{\frac{1}{n}}} = \sqrt[n]{a^{m}}$$</equation>
    <p>This leads us to the following definition.</p>
    <note type="Rational Exponentamnamn">
      <p>For any positive integers m and n,</p>
      <equation>$$a^{\frac{m}{n}} = \left( \sqrt[n]{a} \right)^{m}\quad\text{and}\quad a^{\frac{m}{n}} = \sqrt[n]{a^{m}}$$</equation>
    </note>
    <p>Which form do we use to simplify an expression? We usually take the root first—that way we keep the numbers in the radicand smaller, before raising it to the power indicated.</p>
    <example number="8.30">
      <problem>
        <p>Write with a rational exponent: ⓐ $$\sqrt{y^{3}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( \sqrt[3]{2x} \right)^{4}$$ ⓒ $$\sqrt{\left( \frac{3a}{4b} \right)^{3}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>We want to use $$a^{\frac{m}{n}} = \sqrt[n]{a^{m}}$$ to write each radical in the form $$a^{\frac{m}{n}}.$$</p>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.59">
      <p>Write with a rational exponent: ⓐ $$\sqrt{x^{5}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( \sqrt[4]{3y} \right)^{3}$$ ⓒ $$\sqrt{\left( \frac{2m}{3n} \right)^{5}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.60">
      <p>Write with a rational exponent: ⓐ $$\sqrt[5]{a^{2}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( \sqrt[3]{5ab} \right)^{5}$$ ⓒ $$\sqrt{\left( \frac{7xy}{z} \right)^{3}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Remember that $$a^{\text{−}n} = \frac{1}{a^{n}}.$$ The negative sign in the exponent does not change the sign of the expression.</p>
    <example number="8.31">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$125^{\frac{2}{3}}$$ ⓑ $$16^{- \frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓒ $$32^{- \frac{2}{5}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>We will rewrite the expression as a radical first using the defintion, $$a^{\frac{m}{n}} = \left( \sqrt[n]{a} \right)^{m}.$$ This form lets us take the root first and so we keep the numbers in the radicand smaller than if we used the other form.</p>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of an expression with a fractional exponent by converting it to radical form.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 125^{\frac{2}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The power of the radical is the numerator of the exponent, 2.The index of the radical is the denominator of theexponent, 3.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( \sqrt[3]{125} \right)^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}(5)^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 25$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ We will rewrite each expression first using $$a^{\text{−}n} = \frac{1}{a^{n}}$$ and then change to radical form.</p>
        <table summary="Steps for simplifying the exponential expression 16^(-3/2) using exponent and radical rules.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 16^{- \frac{3}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite using $$a^{\text{−}n} = \frac{1}{a^{n}}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{1}{16^{\frac{3}{2}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Change to radical form. The power of the radical is thenumerator of the exponent, 3. The index is the denominatorof the exponent, 2.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{1}{\left( \sqrt{16} \right)^{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{1}{4^{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{1}{64}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of 32^(-2/5), illustrating conversion from negative fractional exponent to radical form and final evaluation.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{270mu} 32^{- \frac{2}{5}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite using $$a^{\text{−}n} = \frac{1}{a^{n}}.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{270mu}\frac{1}{32^{\frac{2}{5}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Change to radical form.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{270mu}\frac{1}{\left( \sqrt[5]{32} \right)^{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite the radicand as a power.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{270mu}\frac{1}{\left( \sqrt[5]{2^{5}} \right)^{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{270mu}\frac{1}{2^{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{270mu}\frac{1}{4}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.61">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$27^{\frac{2}{3}}$$ ⓑ $$81^{- \frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓒ $$16^{- \frac{3}{4}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.62">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$4^{\frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$27^{- \frac{2}{3}}$$ ⓒ $$625^{- \frac{3}{4}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="8.32">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\text{−}25^{\frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$\text{−}25^{- \frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓒ $$(-25)^{\frac{3}{2}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of a negative number raised to a fractional exponent.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{252mu}\text{−}25^{\frac{3}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite in radical form.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{252mu}\text{−}\left( \sqrt{25} \right)^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{252mu}\text{−}(5)^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{252mu}-125$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of the expression -25^(-3/2) using exponent rules, radical forms, and arithmetic operations.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{234mu}\text{−}25^{- \frac{3}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite using $$a^{\text{−}n} = \frac{1}{a^{n}}.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{234mu}\text{−}\left( \frac{1}{25^{\frac{3}{2}}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite in radical form.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{234mu}\text{−}\left( \frac{1}{\left( \sqrt{25} \right)^{3}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the radical.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{234mu}\text{−}\left( \frac{1}{(5)^{3}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{234mu} - \frac{1}{125}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ</p>
        <table summary="Evaluates the expression (-25)^(3/2), demonstrating the steps to rewrite it in radical form and concluding that it is not a real number.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}(-25)^{\frac{3}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite in radical form.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( \sqrt{-25} \right)^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>There is no real number whose square root$$\text{is}\ -25.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\text{Not a real number.}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.63">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$-16^{\frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$-16^{- \frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓒ $$(-16)^{- \frac{3}{2}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.64">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$-81^{\frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$-81^{- \frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓒ $$(-81)^{- \frac{3}{2}}.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Use the Properties of Exponents to Simplify Expressions with Rational Exponents">
    <p>The same properties of exponents that we have already used also apply to rational exponents. We will list the Properties of Exponenets here to have them for reference as we simplify expressions.</p>
    <note type="Properties of Exponents">
      <p>If a and b are real numbers and m and n are rational numbers, then</p>
      <p>$$\begin{array}{lccrll} \textbf{Product Property} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu} a^{m} \cdot a^{n}} &amp; = &amp; a^{m + n} \\ \textbf{Power Property} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu}\left( a^{m} \right)^{n}} &amp; = &amp; a^{m \cdot n} \\ \textbf{Product to a Power} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu}\left( {ab} \right)^{m}} &amp; = &amp; {a^{m}b^{m}} \\ \textbf{Quotient Property} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu}\frac{a^{m}}{a^{n}}} &amp; = &amp; {a^{m - n},\ a \neq 0} \\ \textbf{Zero Exponent Definition} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu} a^{0}} &amp; = &amp; {1,\ a \neq 0} \\ \textbf{Quotient to a Power Property} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu}\left( \frac{a}{b} \right)^{m}} &amp; = &amp; {\frac{a^{m}}{b^{m}},\ b \neq 0} \\ \textbf{Negative Exponent Property} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\mspace{72mu} a^{\text{−}n}} &amp; = &amp; {\frac{1}{a^{n}},\ a \neq 0} \end{array}$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>We will apply these properties in the next example.</p>
    <example number="8.33">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$x^{\frac{1}{2}} \cdot x^{\frac{5}{6}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( z^{9} \right)^{\frac{2}{3}}$$ ⓒ $$\frac{x^{\frac{1}{3}}}{x^{\frac{5}{3}}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ The Product Property tells us that when we multiply the same base, we add the exponents.</p>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates the step-by-step simplification of an algebraic expression involving fractional exponents by applying the rule for multiplying powers with the same base.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu} x^{\frac{1}{2}} \cdot x^{\frac{5}{6}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The bases are the same, so we add theexponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu} x^{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{5}{6}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Add the fractions.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu} x^{\frac{8}{6}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify the exponent.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{90mu} x^{\frac{4}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ The Power Property tells us that when we raise a power to a power, we multiply the exponents.</p>
        <table summary="Steps for simplifying an exponential expression using the power of a power rule, showing the rule's application and the final result.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu}\left( z^{9} \right)^{\frac{2}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>To raise a power to a power, we multiplythe exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} z^{9 \cdot \frac{2}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{81mu} z^{6}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓒ The Quotient Property tells us that when we divide with the same base, we subtract the exponents.</p>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates the step-by-step simplification of an exponential expression using the quotient rule for exponents.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{x^{\frac{1}{3}}}{x^{\frac{5}{3}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{x^{\frac{1}{3}}}{x^{\frac{5}{3}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>To divide with the same base, we subtractthe exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{1}{x^{\frac{5}{3} - \frac{1}{3}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{1}{x^{\frac{4}{3}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.65">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$x^{\frac{1}{6}} \cdot x^{\frac{4}{3}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( x^{6} \right)^{\frac{4}{3}}$$ ⓒ $$\frac{x^{\frac{2}{3}}}{x^{\frac{5}{3}}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.66">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$y^{\frac{3}{4}} \cdot y^{\frac{5}{8}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( m^{9} \right)^{\frac{2}{9}}$$ ⓒ $$\frac{d^{\frac{1}{5}}}{d^{\frac{6}{5}}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Sometimes we need to use more than one property. In the next example, we will use both the Product to a Power Property and then the Power Property.</p>
    <example number="8.34">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {27u^{\frac{1}{2}}} \right)^{\frac{2}{3}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( {m^{\frac{2}{3}}n^{\frac{1}{2}}} \right)^{\frac{3}{2}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of an algebraic expression involving fractional exponents using exponent properties.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( {27u^{\frac{1}{2}}} \right)^{\frac{2}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>First we use the Product to a PowerProperty.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}(27)^{\frac{2}{3}}\left( u^{\frac{1}{2}} \right)^{\frac{2}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rewrite 27 as a power of 3.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( 3^{3} \right)^{\frac{2}{3}}\left( u^{\frac{1}{2}} \right)^{\frac{2}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>To raise a power to a power, we multiplythe exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( 3^{2} \right)\left( u^{\frac{1}{3}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} 9u^{\frac{1}{3}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="This table illustrates the step-by-step simplification of an algebraic expression involving fractional exponents and the product to a power property.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( {m^{\frac{2}{3}}n^{\frac{1}{2}}} \right)^{\frac{3}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>First we use the Product to a PowerProperty.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( m^{\frac{2}{3}} \right)^{\frac{3}{2}}\left( n^{\frac{1}{2}} \right)^{\frac{3}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>To raise a power to a power, we multiplythe exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} mn^{\frac{3}{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.67">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {32x^{\frac{1}{3}}} \right)^{\frac{3}{5}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( {x^{\frac{3}{4}}y^{\frac{1}{2}}} \right)^{\frac{2}{3}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.68">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\left( {81n^{\frac{2}{5}}} \right)^{\frac{3}{2}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( {a^{\frac{3}{2}}b^{\frac{1}{2}}} \right)^{\frac{4}{3}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>We will use both the Product Property and the Quotient Property in the next example.</p>
    <example number="8.35">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\frac{x^{\frac{3}{4}} \cdot x^{- \frac{1}{4}}}{x^{- \frac{6}{4}}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( \frac{16\ x^{\frac{4}{3}}y^{- \frac{5}{6}}}{x^{- \frac{2}{3}}y^{\frac{1}{6}}} \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of a rational expression using the Product and Quotient Properties of exponents.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{x^{\frac{3}{4}} \cdot x^{- \frac{1}{4}}}{x^{- \frac{6}{4}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Product Property in the numerator,add the exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{x^{\frac{2}{4}}}{x^{- \frac{6}{4}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Quotient Property, subtract theexponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} x^{\frac{8}{4}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu} x^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ Follow the order of operations to simplify inside the parenthese first.</p>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of an algebraic expression involving fractional exponents using properties of exponents.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( \frac{16\ x^{\frac{4}{3}}y^{- \frac{5}{6}}}{x^{- \frac{2}{3}}y^{\frac{1}{6}}} \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Quotient Property, subtract theexponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( \frac{16x^{\frac{6}{3}}}{y^{\frac{6}{6}}} \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\left( \frac{16x^{2}}{y} \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the Product to a Power Property,multiply the exponents.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{72mu}\frac{4x}{y^{\frac{1}{2}}}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.69">
      <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\frac{m^{\frac{2}{3}} \cdot m^{- \frac{1}{3}}}{m^{- \frac{5}{3}}}$$ ⓑ $$\left( \frac{25m^{\frac{1}{6}}n^{\frac{11}{6}}}{m^{\frac{2}{3}}n^{- \frac{1}{6}}} \right)^{\frac{1}{2}}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="8.70">
